From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Walsh <contact.me@thejustinwalsh.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:28:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] llama: LLAMA_LAYER_MAJOR=1, a prompt batch runs layer by
 layer so the host-resident experts of a layer are uploaded once per batch

With the experts in host memory (--n-cpu-moe 48) every offloaded MUL_MAT_ID of every 512-token ubatch uploads the experts
the ubatch routes to (ggml_backend_sched_compute_splits, the used-experts copy): at 512 tokens x 10 of 512 experts that is
nearly all of the 35.45 GB of routed experts per ubatch (33.02 GiB, 48 layers, gguf sizes), and it is the ~75% of a
ubatch's 3,124 ms that no timed node accounts for (the engine profile of 2026-10-02). This runs the batch the other way:
layer 0 for every ubatch of the batch, then layer 1 for every ubatch, ... , so a layer's experts cross PCIe once per
batch (-b: 2,048 tokens = 4 ubatches today, `-b 8192` = 16) instead of once per ubatch. Strata does the same order with
one 8,192-token batch per layer (src/prefill/prefill.cpp:752-856 @ d551edf); here the ubatch stays 512, so the compute
buffer does not grow (3.7 MiB a token at -c 262144 would).

Off unless LLAMA_LAYER_MAJOR is set (default: nothing changes: GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=2 of llama-bench on the A4000, a
generated qwen4exp, is identical to the int0028 build's, 454 splits, 18,560 split/node lines). qwen4exp only; a batch
of one ubatch, more than one sequence is fine, embeddings/pooling/backend samplers/layer-input taps and an MTP-type
context take the old path. 1 = where it gains (a GPU, experts in host memory, op offload, no --prefetch-experts-slots),
2 = wherever it can run (the CPU test). LLAMA_LAYER_MAJOR_SLOTS (default 6 expert slots), _MIN_UBATCHES (2), _DEBUG=1
(one line per batch: ms, bytes uploaded, hits, host time per layer graph).

What changed
- llama-context.cpp decode(): a batch of >= 2 ubatches goes through decode_layer_major(). Every ubatch is applied to the
  memory ONCE, in order; llama_memory_context_i::lm_record() keeps what its graph reads (the K/V and indexer cell range,
  the recurrent head, rs_z and every s_copy id, which also consumes the rollback index of a sequence once);
  lm_seek(i) puts it back for later layers, so layer L of ubatch i is built over exactly the cells and state the
  ubatch-by-ubatch path built it over. Per layer: the graph of that layer for ubatch 0..N-1 (never reused: ~0.04 ms to
  build, ~0.04 to split and allocate, A4000, tiny model), then the next. finish_ubatch() (logits, embeddings, h_nextn
  for the MTP draft) is the old code, called after the last layer of each ubatch.
- llm_graph_params lm_il0/lm_il1/lm_h_in/lm_h_out/lm_tok_off, qwen4exp::graph builds a layer range: past layer 0 it starts
  from rows of a persistent device buffer (2 x 40 KiB a token at hc 4 x n_embd 2560, ping-pong by layer parity), short
  of the last layer it copies the residual into the other one. Inputs a range does not read are not created
  (build_inp_mem_hybrid(attn, rs)).
- ggml-backend.cpp: weight-stationary expert slots (ggml-backend-lm.h). A split whose MUL_MAT_ID reads a host weight
  finds it whole in a slot or uploads it whole (second stream of the device, an event to the compute stream); the driver
  names the next layer's weights right after the first graph of a layer, so their upload overlaps its compute. A slot of
  the current layer is never reused. Counters for the used-experts copy and the whole-weight copy are always kept.
- tests/test-layer-major.cpp, tests/split_gate_up.py: a generated qwen4exp (Gated DeltaNet + QSA layers, hyper
  connections, PLE, MoE), the same batches in two contexts.

Tested (a generated 8-layer qwen4exp, 16-64 experts, not the real model; n = the runs listed)
- CPU, bit-exact logits and byte-identical serialized sequence state (K/V, indexer cells, recurrent state) after a
  prompt batch with an output on every token, a server-style batch (output on the last token only), a rollback of K
  positions with n_rs_seq snapshots and a batch after it, and generated tokens; ubatch 20-128, 1-4 sequences, top-k
  24-131072, f16 and q8_0 K/V, MTP hidden states (llama_set_embeddings_nextn), a batch cancelled in the middle: 48
  runs (6 seeds x 8 configurations).
- A4000 (CUDA0, experts in host memory, f32 and Q4_0, fused and separate gate/up, 3-6 slots): bit-exact too, 19 runs.
  The 6 full ubatches of a 401-token batch copied 288.0 MiB of experts ubatch by ubatch; layer-major uploaded 48.0 MiB
  (24 uploads, 21 of them prefetched, 141 hits): one ubatch's worth, as N = 6 predicts.
- A control that must fail does (the ubatches of every layer past the first taken back to front), and a batch cancelled
  at the Nth abort-callback call is rolled back and decodes again to the reference.

NOT measured: anything on the real model or the 5060 Ti. The gain is an estimate (docs/FLASH-NEXT.md section 12).

